{"type":"message","id":"55d788d5","parentId":"b7f1c5d0","timestamp":"2026-05-24T02:45:56.212Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both headers together didn't work either. The /health endpoint works fine, so the API key is valid for simple requests. The /send endpoint returns error code 1101. Let me try one more time with just the Bearer header that works for GET /history.\n\nWait - I used Bearer with GET and it didn't work for GET either. Let me re-check...\n\nActually looking back, the first GET with Bearer returned \"Unauthorized\". The one with X-API-KEY returned data. So X-API-KEY is the correct header.\n\nBut for POST /send with X-API-KEY I still get error 1101. And the health endpoint just returned \"OK\" without any key - it might not require auth.\n \nLet me compare what's different between the health request and the send request. Health uses GET and returned OK, while send uses POST and returns 1101. This could mean either the POST endpoint requires a different auth method, or it's validating the actual request body for something like a required field that's missing.\n\nThe error code 1101 seems to be an application-level error rather than an auth failure, so the authentication is probably passing. Let me try submitting the request with just the Bearer token to see if that's the missing piece.\n","thinkingSignature":"16d9e9e5e9a7908a3d8bc5058fbac3cfa41de507948898ab39050768e8d403dc"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_9p6u0ak29t2w_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"curl -s -X POST \"https://echo-agent-platform.zhouyating.workers.dev/send\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer echo-a…-key\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"threadId\":\"arbitration\",\"content\":\"[Heartbeat 02:45 UTC]\"}'"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax-portal","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","usage":{"input":158,"output":346,"cacheRead":22272,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":22776,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRea